/* Tiny messages */
static bool msg_tiny_flag = false;
static CONFIDENTIAL uint8_t msg_tiny[MSG_TINY_BFR_SZ];
static uint16_t msg_tiny_id = MSG_TINY_TYPE_ERROR; /* Default to error type */

So duplicate definitions cause compile error (redefinition). The diff also removed the old msg_read_tiny function? Actually later in file after the duplicate definitions, the old msg_read_tiny function is still present unchanged. The new tiny_dispatch is added earlier, but the old msg_read_tiny remains used in handle_usb_rx and handle_debug_usb_rx only if msg_tiny_flag true. However the diff changed those functions to inline the logic and call tiny_dispatch, but they still call msg_read_tiny? Let's see diff for handle_usb_rx: It replaced msg_read_tiny with custom code that extracts msgId, msgSize, checks size, gets entry via message_map_entry, then tiny_dispatch(entry, buf+9, msgSize). So they removed call to msg_read_tiny. Good.

But msg_read_tiny function is still defined but never used (except maybe elsewhere). That's okay but duplicate static variables cause error. Also the earlier added static definitions cause redefinition error. The code originally had static definitions after usb_rx_helper. The PR moved them earlier but didn't delete the later ones. So compile fails.

We should check if any other issues: tiny_dispatch uses pb_parse(entry, msg, msg_size, msg_tiny). pb_parse expects entry->fields etc. It returns bool. It sets msg_tiny_id = entry->msg_id; but entry->msg_id is MessageType enum, not necessarily same as tiny type? In original code, msg_tiny_id stored the raw msgId (MessageType). That's fine.

Potential missing include for CONFIDENTIAL macro? Already present earlier.

Thus the PR introduces duplicate definitions and likely fails compilation. Should decline.

